WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.090
- Good afternoon everybody
welcome to this year's

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.550
Tanner Lectures at Yale University,

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.560
hosted by the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.750
I'm Gary Tomlinson I'm the
director of the Whitney.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.210
We're gathered of course
to hear from Achille Mbembe

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.550
who is a research professor

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.630
at the University of the Witwatersrand

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.990
in Johannesburg, South Africa.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.130
And more specifically,
at the Wits Institute

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.450
for Social and Economic
Research, WISER as it is known

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.120
and we're delighted by the way to have

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.800
the director of WISER professor
Sarah Nuttall with us,

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.519
also today, Sarah.

00:00:39.519 --> 00:00:42.769
(audience applauding)

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.740
I've known Professor Mbembe,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.960
since we were colleagues at
the University of Pennsylvania

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.330
some 25 years ago and is a huge pleasure

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:55.670
for me to welcome him to this stage today.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.450
I know we are in for today,
tomorrow and Thursday

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:01.550
not only an intellectual
feast, but something more

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.300
since Professor Mbembe
has in his many writings,

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.920
it's not too much to say,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.000
recast the terms of post-colonial
analysis and critique.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.430
This achievement is particularly marked

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.320
in his most recent book,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.330
but one, Critique of Black Reason

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.470
where he leads us to understand blackness,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.760
not only as a project of the imagination

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.400
shaped by half a
millennium of subjugation,

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.550
enslavement, exploitation, murder

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.690
but also as the condition
that all the worlds

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:28.620
subaltern people are subjected to today

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.703
by the forces of neoliberal capital.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.010
But I leave further
introduction of Professor Mbembe

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.610
to my colleague Michael Denning.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:40.200
To me it's left to outline
the schedule of Tanner events

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.050
over the next two days
and just say a few words

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.590
about the Tanner as an institution.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.290
This afternoon, we hear the first

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.360
of Professor Mbembe two lectures,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.710
afterwards you're all
invited to a reception

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.060
here at the Whitney, it's
straight down the hall

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.143
directly behind you.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.550
Tomorrow again at five
o'clock Professor Mbembe

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.030
will present his second lecture,

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.440
and after that there will be a short

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.750
question and answer session.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.540
And then on Thursday
morning finally, at 10:30,

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.730
we reconvene for a discussion
among Professor Mbembe,

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.770
Professor Denning and
another Yale professor

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:11.843
Inderpal Grewal.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.010
At that session there will
be additional opportunity

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.823
for questions from the audience.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.180
As to the Tanner Lectures on Human Values,

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.350
they were established
by the American scholar,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.200
industrialist and philanthropist
Obert Clark Tanner

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.030
and his wife Grace Tanner.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.930
For many years a professor
at the University of Utah,

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.500
and a scholar of the New Testament

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.820
educated at Harvard and Stanford,

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.190
Tanner also founded in
1927, early in his adulthood

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.910
a jewelry company in Salt Lake City.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.390
The company later
expanded into broad areas

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.620
of human resource consulting

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.240
and employee recognition programs.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.810
They made trophies and placards and so on

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.340
for 25 years of service
and things like that,

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.690
and from that made a whole lot of money,

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:55.210
and it is on the base of that corporation

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.250
that the Tanner foundation

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:02.040
and Grace Tanner's
philanthropic efforts are built.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.640
These efforts include today
the Tanner Lectures at Yale,

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.280
and at a number of other
universities in the United States,

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.963
Great Britain and beyond.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:12.570
The purpose Obert Tanner envisioned

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.070
for the Tanner Lectures is to advance

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.430
and reflect upon scholarly
and scientific learning

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:18.230
relating to human values,

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:19.700
in creating the lectureships

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.970
professor Tanner knowingly
left his purview very broad,

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.367
he wrote, "I see these
lectures simply as a search

00:03:25.367 --> 00:03:27.327
"for a better understanding
of human behavior

00:03:27.327 --> 00:03:28.857
"and human values."

00:03:28.857 --> 00:03:30.247
"This understanding may be pursued

00:03:30.247 --> 00:03:31.277
"for its own intrinsic worth,

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:34.147
"but it may also eventually
have practical consequences

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:37.227
"for the quality of
personal and social life."

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.700
Appointment as a Tanner lecturer

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.860
is a recognition of uncommon achievement

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.580
on outstanding abilities in the area

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.730
of human values and studies,

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.010
and we are honored to add Professor Mbembe

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:50.990
to the list of distinguished
scholars and humanists

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:53.780
whom Yale has so recognized.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.910
At Yale, the Tanner lecturers are selected

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.370
by the President, in
consultation with the director

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.570
and executive committee of
the Whitney Humanities Center

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.530
so we're also indebted to
Yale President Peter Salovey

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:04.830
who will be here tomorrow night

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:08.550
for his assistance in bringing
today's speaker to us.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.270
Now, it's my pleasure
to welcome to the podium

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:11.490
Michael Denning William,

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.950
our keen and junior professor

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:14.420
of American Studies and English

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.706
to introduce our speaker, Michael.

00:04:16.706 --> 00:04:19.873
(audience applauding)

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.357
- Thank you Gary,

00:04:27.357 --> 00:04:31.160
it's a great pleasure to
welcome Achille Mbembe

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.140
back to Yale to deliver the Tanner Lecture

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.993
Borders in the Age of Networks.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.990
I won't repeat that he's
a research professor

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.980
with Witwatersrand, I'll just say,

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:47.980
I last introduced Achille
to a Yale audience in 2007,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:53.340
and in the decades since he
has published three major books

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.840
and I thought start with those,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.590
rather than going way back.

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:59.250
The first in 2010,

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.890
was Sortir de la Grande Nuit,

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.400
an allusion to Fanon's call

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.430
to shake off the great mantle of night.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:12.090
Essai sur l'Afrique Décolonisée,

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.330
Essay, on decolonized Africa.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.310
Not yet translated into English

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:20.010
it opens with an
extraordinarily, powerful,

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:25.010
autobiographical narrative of
a country and three cities.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.320
First, and please excuse
my halting translations.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.130
I hope they're accurate.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:36.080
Born in the shadow of a
country without a proper name,

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.860
Cameroon the name given
by Portuguese seamen

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:42.680
marveling at the abundance of shrimp,

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.290
camaroe, in the river.

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:47.757
And born in the shadow of and I quote,

00:05:47.757 --> 00:05:50.400
"The first age of post colonialism."

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.590
In it's childhood and adolescence,

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:56.710
and in the shadow of songs of lamentation

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:00.760
for the disavowed martyrs of
the anti-colonial struggle,

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.890
and the schoolroom anthems

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:06.063
of the post-colonial nation and potentate.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:10.120
Then as when intellectuals, and I quote

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.950
Since the time of Fanon,
Cesaire and Senghor

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:17.310
having taken his masters at
the University of Yaounde

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.730
he left for Paris where he took

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:22.970
his doctorate at the Sorbonne and New York

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:24.710
where he taught at Columbia.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:28.780
And where I won't summarize
his luminous pages

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:31.350
on what those two cities meant to him.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:36.030
He became a historian not only
of the resistance in Cameroon

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.170
editing a collection of the writings

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:39.677
of the martyred Rueben Umnioba

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:44.530
and publishing in 1996,

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:48.040
The Birth of the Resistance
in South Cameroon.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.680
But of the surpluses of
post-colonial Africa,

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:55.090
and he forged the remarkable dialectic

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:58.800
of historical reflection
and critical theory

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.700
that has come to characterize his style,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.160
first on the post colony
translated into English

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.763
published 2000 and translated
into English in 2001.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.680
But then in the last
year of the 20th century

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.830
as he puts it, he arrived in South Africa.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:23.377
In the shadow and I'm quoting
here again the Maelstrom,

00:07:23.377 --> 00:07:26.787
"In the shadow of the
Maelstrom that was apartheid,

00:07:26.787 --> 00:07:31.757
"it's statues still standing
its streets not yet renamed

00:07:31.757 --> 00:07:36.027
"in a city Johannesburg the
most modern, most powerful

00:07:36.027 --> 00:07:39.203
"and most racially mixed
of African metropolis

00:07:39.203 --> 00:07:43.817
" is a new frontier of the
continent where one finds

00:07:43.817 --> 00:07:47.830
"almost all the
nationalities of the world."

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:51.990
A migrant to Johannesburg,
it is from Johannesburg

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:56.010
that Achelle Mbembe has
over the last two decades

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.830
reimagined our world.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:02.070
In his account of Africa's
attempt to shake off

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.230
the great mantle of night,

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:07.410
in his extraordinary 2013 book,

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.310
Critique of Black Reason,

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:12.040
which has just been
translated into English.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:16.630
A philosophical history of
the invention of black reason.

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:19.720
The Western consciousness
of Africa and blackness

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:23.170
in the shadow of the slave
trade and colonialism,

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.560
of the reversal of that black reason

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.420
into the black reason that is
the polyglot internationalism

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:31.690
of the modern black imaginary.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:35.940
In figures from Garvey and
Dubois to Fanon and Mandela,

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:40.323
and finally to the Becoming
Black of the World.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:44.860
And most recently in the 2016 book,

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:47.400
The Politics of Enmity,

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:50.370
a chapter of which has
been recently translated

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:54.900
in radical philosophy, a
reflection on a world dominated

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.660
by the desire for an enemy.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:01.700
A desire for apartheid,
a desire for separation

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:05.150
and exclusion and walls, and the new forms

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:09.020
of nano racism and hydraulic racism,

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:11.880
that that world has shaped.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.390
Throughout all his work,

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:17.220
he has been haunted by death and corpses.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:21.540
Necropolitics has been one of
his most influential essays.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:23.740
And death stretches across his work

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:26.330
from the cemeteries in Cameroon

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:29.920
to the amazing essay
on Mandela's mortality,

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:32.293
written together with Sara Nuttall.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.580
However, recalling the songs

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.090
of lamentation of his grandmother,

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:41.240
for the son assassinated
in the resistance,

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:46.240
he writes that, these songs
served to accompany his shadow

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:50.810
and to open for him away
to a possible repose

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:55.230
in compensation for the
unspeakable injustice

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:58.133
the victim experienced after his demise.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:03.120
In a similar way,
Achille Mbembe's writings

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.920
accompany the shadows of the dead

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.290
and undead of our world,

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:11.930
and open a way to a possible repose,

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:14.740
and in common as he calls it

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:17.840
beyond the unspeakable injustices

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:20.660
of a society of enmity.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:23.450
Please join me in welcoming Achille Mbembe

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:26.250
for the first of his two Tanner lectures,

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.883
The New Global Mobility Regime.

00:10:30.309 --> 00:10:33.559
(audience applauding)

00:10:52.658 --> 00:10:56.960
- I would like to thank

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.290
Gary and the University

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:03.313
for a very gracious invitation.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:10.490
As Gary said, we were both colleagues,

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:12.040
many years ago.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:15.960
He said 25 years and I suddenly realized

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:17.820
that time has been

00:11:19.584 --> 00:11:21.160
passing rather quickly,

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:24.120
but as you can testify,

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:27.333
Gary always looks very young,

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.840
both physically and in his mind.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:33.693
Gary was and has been all along,

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:39.000
his work has been a great
inspiration for a lot of us.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:43.063
I would also like to extend
my gratitude to Michael.

00:11:47.081 --> 00:11:48.880
I like Michael very, very much,

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:52.210
I know he likes me too as well,

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:55.973
you could see with his introduction,

00:11:57.070 --> 00:12:01.493
and I'm very glad my wife
Sarah Nuttall is here.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.963
Perhaps, more than any other,

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:12.823
Ours is an epoch of fantasies.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:17.760
One such fantasy is that of

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:20.163
total security,

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:24.840
or perfectly controlled,

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:27.870
or tightened borders,

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:31.890
the dream of impenetrable

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:35.240
and insurmountable limits

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:38.880
which is the same thing as the dream of

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:43.173
a domain without an outside.

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:48.490
A domain whose walls
cannot be broken through

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:53.920
and whose boundaries can never be crossed,

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:58.920
if only because they are
continuously redrawn,

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.313
displaced and pushed back.

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:08.940
So as the twin logics of order
and security on the one hand

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.650
and freedom and movement on the other

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:16.793
confront one another,

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:20.994
borders almost everywhere

00:13:20.994 --> 00:13:24.643
are increasingly found
in unexpected places.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:28.013
In the not-so-distant past,

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:32.320
borders have always been generative

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:37.293
of new modes of power, and
a new political reason.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:43.050
Such was the case in 1884, 1885

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:48.850
when meeting in Berlin
for what was then known

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:51.683
as the Congo Conference.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:57.560
14 European powers
divided Africa, among them

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:00.263
for the purpose of colonization.

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:06.390
This they did, by carving out

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:10.120
the entire continent and by superimposing

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:15.120
on pre-existing political
entities and cultures,

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:19.260
a hotspot of geometric boundaries

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:23.903
that divided it into
50 aberrant countries.

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:30.360
As clearly pointed out in the
general acts of the conference

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:33.031
this was all I quote,

00:14:33.031 --> 00:14:36.781
"In the name of free
trade and civilization."

00:14:37.628 --> 00:14:40.694
As Article II of that general act

00:14:40.694 --> 00:14:42.730
of the conference specifies

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:43.743
I quote again,

00:14:44.997 --> 00:14:47.727
"All flags without distinction

00:14:47.727 --> 00:14:51.657
"of nationality shall have free access

00:14:51.657 --> 00:14:53.787
"to the whole of the coastline

00:14:53.787 --> 00:14:57.020
"of the territories above enumerated."

00:14:57.020 --> 00:14:58.510
Enumerated in Article I,

00:14:59.527 --> 00:15:03.327
"To the rivers there running into the sea

00:15:03.327 --> 00:15:06.383
"to all the waters of the
Congo and its effluence,

00:15:07.247 --> 00:15:11.377
"including the lakes, and to all the ports

00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:15.087
"situated on the banks of these waters

00:15:15.087 --> 00:15:19.607
"as well as to all the
canals which may in future

00:15:19.607 --> 00:15:24.297
"be constructed with intent
to unite the water courses

00:15:24.297 --> 00:15:27.367
"or lakes within the entire area

00:15:27.367 --> 00:15:30.087
"of the territories
described in article one."

00:15:30.087 --> 00:15:31.403
End of quote.

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:37.040
The said Article I, starts
with the following statement,

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:37.963
I quote again.

00:15:38.987 --> 00:15:43.703
"The trade of all nations
shall enjoy complete freedom.

00:15:46.357 --> 00:15:49.897
"Follows geographical specification

00:15:49.897 --> 00:15:54.830
"which is at the same time
a naming of a large boundary

00:15:55.743 --> 00:15:58.537
"and the claiming of a
huge chunk of territory

00:15:58.537 --> 00:16:01.187
"called the basin of the Congo."

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:03.663
And so it goes.

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:08.870
A new mode of power,
a new political reason

00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:10.900
but even more crucially

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:14.580
a claim made in relation to

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:17.640
an assemblage of territories,

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.450
territories made of rivers, of mountains,

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:23.710
ridges and watersheds.

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:27.710
Lakes, maritime zones, lines,

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:30.110
parallels and latitudes.

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:35.110
Points of demarkation and
assemblage of territories,

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:38.233
power is seeking to take possession of.

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:44.900
So at the beginning, or at
least in our colonial experience

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:47.293
the resident of the border,

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:52.210
is to attend to the problem

00:16:53.484 --> 00:16:56.853
of the distribution of the earth.

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:03.020
How much the earth of land be distributed?

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:05.703
To whom does the earth belong?

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:10.963
Who can lay what type of
claim to what part of it?

00:17:12.340 --> 00:17:15.950
To whom should be allotted what share?

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:19.653
Who determines its
distribution or partition?

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:24.890
In its colonial roots, geopolitics

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:27.710
is about the multiple distribution

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:32.710
of populations, not only human
on the body of the earth.

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:37.150
It is about the modes of occupation

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:39.260
and the distribution of the earth,

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:43.170
and the manner in which these modes affect

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:46.590
the vital forces of our kinds of beings,

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:48.283
human and non-human.

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:54.800
Yesterday borders used
to manifest themselves

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:56.033
in two major ways.

00:17:56.950 --> 00:18:01.950
First, as tangible, physical features,

00:18:01.990 --> 00:18:04.853
usually located at the
edge of state territory.

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:09.770
And second, as sites where discretionary

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:12.610
sovereign decisions were made,

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:15.410
in relation to whether to permit

00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:19.710
or to exclude what,
whom, under what terms.

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:22.310
Nowadays, they are ever more mobile

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:27.240
complex, differentiated, dispersed

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:31.163
and fracturelized just
as they remain contested.

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:36.220
As more and more people
find themselves on the move,

00:18:36.220 --> 00:18:39.290
new laws regulations, bureaucracies

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:43.920
and procedures are produced
in a renewed attempt

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:47.620
at controlling and
regulating the movements

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:51.660
through and across the
border escapes of our world,

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:55.680
and in an effort at setting
the terms under which

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:59.470
foreign bodies might enter, reside,

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:03.360
work, settle and perhaps integrate

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.573
in countries other than their own.

00:19:06.590 --> 00:19:09.350
Migration policy which is mostly

00:19:09.350 --> 00:19:12.190
about the control of human mobility,

00:19:12.190 --> 00:19:17.190
is once again seen as a
key index of sovereignty

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:20.453
in a world of flows, risks and threats.

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:24.900
Furthermore, border work,

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:29.900
work of instituting, enacting, boundaries,

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:33.620
understood mostly as powers highly

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:37.950
and even imperfect and yet incessant labor

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:40.810
to contain mobility, border work

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:45.810
is evermore incorporated
into everyday ways of life.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:50.220
It is enhanced by
numerous security schemes

00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:54.020
and technologies supposedly configured

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:55.803
to operate objectively.

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:03.960
Objectively, that is without
prejudice or discrimination.

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:07.740
Such security technologies are enlisted

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:12.690
in what is increasingly an
automated calculation of threats

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:15.273
the key feature of our times.

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:20.030
They are configured in
such a way as to track

00:20:20.030 --> 00:20:24.570
unveil and sort, that which is hidden,

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:28.410
that which must be detected and inspected,

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:31.863
that which is out of
place or unauthorized.

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:37.570
Concealed identities, apparent movements

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:41.460
categories of potential enemies,

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:46.293
actual human bodies in this
age of generalized suspicion.

00:20:47.720 --> 00:20:52.720
Indeed, this is also an
epoch when the human body

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:56.490
or more precisely that
body that is not mine

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:00.930
re-emerges as potentially dangerous

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:04.400
and meshed as it is with various figures

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:06.890
of threat and insecurity.

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:09.760
Threat to cultural identity,

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:12.230
threat to the welfare of state,

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:15.580
threat to public order and even

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:19.220
an existential threat to our way of life

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:20.813
and our way of being.

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:26.490
According to the new,
old security rationale,

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:30.280
although everybody is
potentially dangerous

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:32.400
until proven otherwise,

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:36.440
some categories of bodies and persons

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:40.483
carry almost by definition
more risks than others.

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:44.040
Even at the most vulnerable state,

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:47.830
they fundamentally endanger our lives.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:52.780
As such they must be the
object of renewed profiling

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:57.780
classification, and
isolation or even eliminated

00:21:57.830 --> 00:22:00.810
in line with a long history in modernity

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:04.360
that ties together the targeting,

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:06.780
quarantining, restriction

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:10.030
and even extermination of populations

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:13.180
that have been marked as abnormal

00:22:13.180 --> 00:22:15.993
or have been profoundly dishonored.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:20.200
The obsession with the
borders of the human body

00:22:21.130 --> 00:22:23.860
in particular the borders

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:26.570
of that body that is not mine,

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:31.570
has in turn unleashed a
renewed algorithmic production

00:22:31.640 --> 00:22:36.430
of its nude image by fully body scanners

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:40.070
and examination screens.

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:44.480
At a time when security
is equated with visibility

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:46.823
and concealment with threat.

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:53.130
What the body looks
like, what its parts are,

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:56.430
what are its shapes and forms?

00:22:56.430 --> 00:23:00.080
What objects is it concealing?

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:02.590
And what others should delete.

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:04.100
These are questions,

00:23:04.100 --> 00:23:06.680
the new matrix of threat and risk

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:10.083
is forcing us to ask with urgency.

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:14.510
So over the last 15 years,

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:18.150
a huge amount of empirical work

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.050
has been done in relation

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:23.300
to both borders and migration,

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:26.623
and it is not my intention
to rehearse it here.

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:31.060
The wealth of data already accumulated

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:35.450
has helped sharpen our
understanding of mobility

00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:38.280
and of borders, of capture,

00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:40.570
immobility and flight.

00:23:40.570 --> 00:23:42.710
It has also highlighted

00:23:42.710 --> 00:23:45.750
the extent to which various processes

00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:48.270
are unfolding simultaneously,

00:23:48.270 --> 00:23:51.660
the freeing of internal mobility for some

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:56.813
and the hardening of external
boundaries for others.

00:23:58.090 --> 00:24:02.330
Phenomenon such as the re-specialization

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:04.010
of state sovereignty,

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:08.250
the securitization of migration,

00:24:08.250 --> 00:24:12.190
the emerging of criminal justice

00:24:12.190 --> 00:24:14.890
and immigration policing.

00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:19.890
The extraterritorialization
of certain practices

00:24:20.337 --> 00:24:22.993
related to migration management.

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:27.260
These and other processes
have been the object

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:31.053
of extensive and careful descriptions.

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:35.453
Drawing from this material,

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:39.410
it seems to me it's now possible

00:24:39.410 --> 00:24:42.520
to rethink what is at stake

00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:44.540
when the biopolitics

00:24:45.550 --> 00:24:48.320
of mobility intersects

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:50.893
with the geopolitics of our times.

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:53.560
The kind of political reason

00:24:53.560 --> 00:24:56.360
that is produced at this intersection.

00:24:56.360 --> 00:24:58.860
In other words, what is it

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:02.110
that contemporary migratory struggles

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:06.320
tell us about the border
regimes of our times

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:07.930
and the apparatuses of control,

00:25:07.930 --> 00:25:12.030
and capture of bodies, capture of motion,

00:25:12.030 --> 00:25:17.010
capture of speed and ultimately
capture of life itself

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:18.663
in the current conjuncture.

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.570
These are some of the questions

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:25.783
I would like to examine
during these lectures.

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:30.280
I will be using at times interchangeably

00:25:30.280 --> 00:25:32.710
such terms as borders,

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:36.540
migration, mobility, movement.

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:40.710
These are obviously technical concepts,

00:25:40.710 --> 00:25:42.920
borders are at the front line

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:45.200
of present-day geopolitics,

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:47.000
they are also the sites where

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:51.740
sovereignty is the most
subject to reanimation.

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:54.680
To be sure the long-established concept

00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:57.250
of territorial, physical

00:25:57.250 --> 00:25:59.910
or political boundary of a state,

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:02.610
still has relevance in a world

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:06.810
in which lines and
cleaves and compartments

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.320
have not entirely disappeared.

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:12.900
The lines that continue to separate us

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:16.897
are still there, but boundaries or borders

00:26:16.897 --> 00:26:21.243
and no longer conceived
merely as walls or fences.

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:24.870
Even when they manifest themselves

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:27.410
as tangible physical features,

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:30.380
located at the edge of state territory

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:35.370
they are increasingly
understood, as a contingent

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:38.610
assemblage of social practices

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:41.460
subject to change.

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:44.240
They are increasingly understood

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:48.290
as relational arrangements of bodies

00:26:48.290 --> 00:26:52.513
in spaces, and not only as, as containers.

00:26:56.550 --> 00:26:59.173
In fact, as argued by many,

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:02.620
the discretionary sovereign decisions

00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:06.043
that are taken at borders,

00:27:09.020 --> 00:27:13.100
ultimately the result
of various interactions

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:17.530
between border guards, detection devices,

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:21.883
sniffer dogs, humans, non-humans.

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:28.490
Bordering actions or
practices in that sense,

00:27:28.490 --> 00:27:31.570
represent a provisional stabilization

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:35.740
of various forces and devices.

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:40.673
Forces with competing
understandings of threat,

00:27:42.270 --> 00:27:45.440
forces whose function is to enact

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:50.440
or perform security in the face of cunning

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:55.373
and supposedly merciless enemy others.

00:27:56.620 --> 00:27:59.653
And so a border in this sense,

00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:04.820
is the result of how
mobility controlle regime's

00:28:04.820 --> 00:28:08.440
use difference to constitute

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:11.120
different spatial practices.

00:28:11.120 --> 00:28:14.150
So, the point I'm trying to make is that,

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:15.380
as a matter of fact,

00:28:15.380 --> 00:28:20.380
there is no homogenizing
border logic, there isn't.

00:28:22.670 --> 00:28:24.240
Difference and territoriality

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:28.810
are always practiced in
fractured and passion ways,

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:31.210
at least that is one thing we draw

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:36.010
from the voluminous amount of research

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:37.363
I was referring to.

00:28:41.190 --> 00:28:44.763
This having been said and taken together,

00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:48.860
these terms, borders, mobility,

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:50.103
movement and so on.

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:53.593
These terms all mean one thing.

00:28:56.050 --> 00:29:00.940
The multiple distributions
of human populations

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:02.800
on the body of the earth,

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:05.513
that's how I understand these terms.

00:29:06.520 --> 00:29:08.553
They all refer to the same thing.

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:13.470
The monitoring of the
movements of populations

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:17.550
that stipulate on the
surface of the earth,

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:21.083
or that happened to settle
somewhere for various reasons.

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:25.150
But in my understanding,

00:29:25.150 --> 00:29:26.720
what is ultimately at stake

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:30.053
in the current planetary
politics of mobility,

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:35.393
is the drawing of the
new limits of the earth.

00:29:37.330 --> 00:29:41.170
In this context, the
main function of borders

00:29:41.170 --> 00:29:44.180
is precisely to push outside,

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:46.360
to throw outside

00:29:48.050 --> 00:29:49.843
that body that is not mine.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:53.757
Or to put it in Heidegger's terms,

00:29:53.757 --> 00:29:55.063
"The wrong people,

00:29:56.567 --> 00:29:58.570
"with the wrong blood."

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:00.293
That's Heidegger's expression.

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:07.390
In this sense, the border
has the status of law

00:30:08.316 --> 00:30:12.770
to be sure border does not
only exclude or separate,

00:30:12.770 --> 00:30:15.363
it can also connect, but overall,

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:22.100
a border is in the last
instance always conceived

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:25.483
in its disjunctive use.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:31.230
It always refers, in the last instance

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:34.940
to those populations
coming from the outside,

00:30:34.940 --> 00:30:37.940
and in relation to which it must act

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:42.513
as a filter, or which
it actually threatens.

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:48.550
So, I start from the assumption that

00:30:48.550 --> 00:30:52.573
any form of mobility finds
its limit at the border,

00:30:53.930 --> 00:30:57.470
the border is that which
interrupts movement or motion

00:30:58.758 --> 00:31:01.330
and in modernity, border control

00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:04.420
has played two decisive functions,

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:06.410
has been a key device

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:09.223
in the planetary
management of populations.

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:15.310
It has also been a key vector

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:18.100
for the configuration of various degrees

00:31:18.100 --> 00:31:22.350
of subjecthood, flow and
distribution of bodies,

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:25.840
the constitution of
subjects without rights.

00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:28.350
And a preeminent sight

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:30.383
for the execution of state violence.

00:31:32.020 --> 00:31:35.993
The limit where life matters the most.

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:43.120
So, the questions I'm
asking are the following,

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:48.510
to what new existences,
do borders bear witness

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:49.810
in our contemporary world?

00:31:51.230 --> 00:31:55.380
Why, in the confrontation with borders,

00:31:55.380 --> 00:31:59.040
why do so many people,

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:03.000
why are they compelled
towards experimenting

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:04.853
at the limits of the livable?

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:10.010
This way of framing the issue

00:32:10.010 --> 00:32:13.360
is markedly different
from the three approaches

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:16.970
that have of late dominated
debates on mobility

00:32:16.970 --> 00:32:18.993
in the age of borders and networks.

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:22.670
Of the various strands that have of late

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:25.290
dominated such debates,

00:32:25.290 --> 00:32:27.963
three in particular should be highlighted.

00:32:28.990 --> 00:32:31.500
The first trend, centered around

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:35.010
the concept of precarity

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:37.710
or precarious lives,

00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:42.010
has sought to delineate
the interconnections

00:32:42.010 --> 00:32:46.000
between neoliberal work
and welfare regimes.

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:49.300
Immigration controls and exploitation

00:32:49.300 --> 00:32:54.123
or hyper exploitation of
international migrant labor.

00:32:55.930 --> 00:32:59.390
Today, indeed more than
at any other moment

00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:01.360
of our recent history,

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:04.150
migrants are centrally implicated

00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:07.290
in highly precarious work experiences

00:33:07.290 --> 00:33:09.990
at the bottom end of labor markets.

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:13.750
Literally, in western
capitalist countries,

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:17.220
but also in various
parts of the global south

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:22.220
as the case in South Africa,
as the case in Gulf countries

00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:23.803
and so forth and so on.

00:33:25.050 --> 00:33:28.650
The concept of precarity
has been mobilized

00:33:28.650 --> 00:33:31.830
in an effort to describe
those life world's

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:34.940
epitomized by migrant existence,

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:37.830
an existence that is inflected

00:33:37.830 --> 00:33:40.393
with uncertainty and instability.

00:33:41.610 --> 00:33:44.640
It is a concept that has been understood

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:48.413
both as a descriptor and as a condition.

00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:52.140
Condition, let me repeat

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:56.530
which itself is a consequence
of neoliberal globalization,

00:33:56.530 --> 00:34:00.170
and ready transformations
in the world of work

00:34:00.170 --> 00:34:02.330
and precarity in this instance,

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:05.650
has been used as a key
explanatary framework

00:34:05.650 --> 00:34:08.683
for new forms of workplace exploitation.

00:34:10.600 --> 00:34:13.270
The idea that the life of the migrant

00:34:13.270 --> 00:34:17.640
at the bottom end of the labor ladder,

00:34:17.640 --> 00:34:20.410
is the metaphor of precarious life.

00:34:20.410 --> 00:34:23.940
This idea refers to something more

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:27.470
than a position in the labor market.

00:34:27.470 --> 00:34:29.420
Precarity has also been understood

00:34:29.420 --> 00:34:33.690
as something intertwined,
with other areas of life

00:34:33.690 --> 00:34:36.430
something almost existential

00:34:36.430 --> 00:34:39.900
which speaks to the inability to predict

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:44.210
one's fate, or to enjoy

00:34:44.210 --> 00:34:46.150
the degree of stability,

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:49.380
on which to build social relations

00:34:49.380 --> 00:34:51.143
and feelings of affection.

00:34:52.340 --> 00:34:54.470
Precarity has finally been understood

00:34:54.470 --> 00:34:57.800
as a condition of structural insecurity,

00:34:57.800 --> 00:35:01.720
as a consequence of differential exposure

00:35:01.720 --> 00:35:05.000
to violence and suffering that emanates

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:06.890
from socio-political context.

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:09.290
That's the first way in which

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:12.390
the question of mobility,
borders, migrancy

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:14.640
has been dealt with through the lens

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:17.567
of this concept of precarity.

00:35:18.940 --> 00:35:21.750
A second strand of debates on migration

00:35:21.750 --> 00:35:24.790
has focused on emerging geographies

00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:29.160
of detention, imprisonment
and confinement.

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:33.193
In other words, the cultural
landscapes of our world.

00:35:34.470 --> 00:35:36.217
The idea here is that

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:40.260
a migrant, a body on the move

00:35:42.030 --> 00:35:44.003
in a way that is not authorized,

00:35:44.960 --> 00:35:49.050
is almost by definition likely to end up

00:35:49.050 --> 00:35:51.513
in one form or another of a prison.

00:35:53.110 --> 00:35:56.070
To be detained, confined, deported

00:35:56.940 --> 00:36:01.303
is part of the options he
or she permanently faces.

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:05.140
The driving concept in this case,

00:36:05.140 --> 00:36:08.363
has been that of
categorical vulnerability.

00:36:10.130 --> 00:36:15.130
Indeed, during 2009 alone, in the US,

00:36:15.920 --> 00:36:19.210
approximately 380,000 people

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:21.800
spent time in the vast

00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:25.363
and continuosly expanding
migrant detention system.

00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:29.440
According to Detention Watch Network,

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:32.280
this system consisted then,

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:36.360
of approximately 350 facilities

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:39.040
operating at an annual cost

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:41.073
of more than $1.7 billion.

00:36:43.610 --> 00:36:46.570
In other countries, such as the UK,

00:36:46.570 --> 00:36:48.760
the use of migrant detention

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:52.833
in the form of Asylum
screening units, has expanded.

00:36:54.140 --> 00:36:56.230
Across the European Union,

00:36:56.230 --> 00:37:00.070
detention facilities have
similarly proliferated

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:04.200
and now, number in the hundreds

00:37:04.200 --> 00:37:06.710
while Australia has intensified

00:37:06.710 --> 00:37:09.423
detention practices, on and offshore.

00:37:10.590 --> 00:37:13.510
In short, the world has witnessed

00:37:13.510 --> 00:37:15.470
a significant expansion,

00:37:15.470 --> 00:37:18.873
of landscapes of detention
over the last decade.

00:37:20.240 --> 00:37:23.250
So has been the growth
of detention structures

00:37:24.250 --> 00:37:28.190
along transnational routes,
travelled by migrants

00:37:28.190 --> 00:37:31.190
in the journeys through northern Africa,

00:37:31.190 --> 00:37:34.763
Eastern Europe, Indonesia
and Central America.

00:37:36.170 --> 00:37:40.050
Simultaneously, detention and deportation

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:44.660
have become more than ever
interlocked industries.

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:47.890
In this context, an important strand

00:37:47.890 --> 00:37:51.340
of studies on borders and mobility,

00:37:51.340 --> 00:37:54.940
has been preoccupied
with the extent to which

00:37:54.940 --> 00:37:57.030
the categorical vulnerability

00:37:57.030 --> 00:38:00.140
to which migrants and undocumented people

00:38:00.140 --> 00:38:02.790
are subjected, to what extent

00:38:02.790 --> 00:38:05.353
is this category legally produced.

00:38:09.450 --> 00:38:11.940
Then there is a third strand,

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:14.970
which has focused on the warehousing

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:18.930
of refugees and asylum seekers.

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:22.890
Here too, the number
of people in the world

00:38:22.890 --> 00:38:25.230
fleeing conditions of war,

00:38:25.230 --> 00:38:30.100
violence, disasters, economic distress

00:38:30.100 --> 00:38:32.923
and environmental collapse
has been swelling.

00:38:34.690 --> 00:38:39.370
In 2014, the number of
forcibly displaced people

00:38:39.370 --> 00:38:42.360
in the world, surpassed the record number

00:38:43.860 --> 00:38:46.280
of people displaced in the aftermath

00:38:46.280 --> 00:38:47.543
of the Second World War.

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:53.630
I think the latest figures, we have had,

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:54.630
last year (mumbles),

00:38:59.916 --> 00:39:02.730
we are told that there
are 65 million people.

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:07.630
65 million refugees in search of asylum

00:39:07.630 --> 00:39:10.443
in our world as we speak.

00:39:15.810 --> 00:39:18.680
From whatever angle one examines it,

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:23.680
the border therefore remains
a bio political space.

00:39:24.690 --> 00:39:26.760
And in a number of incidences,

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:31.193
border crossing has become
a matter of life and death.

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:36.623
In such a context,
humanitarian border works goal,

00:39:37.937 --> 00:39:40.510
is not to abolish border controls

00:39:40.510 --> 00:39:42.340
or to contest the border

00:39:42.340 --> 00:39:44.940
as a space of exercise of sovereign power,

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:49.790
but to turn it into a space both of rescue

00:39:49.790 --> 00:39:54.453
and of provision of basic emergency care.

00:39:57.390 --> 00:40:00.230
The rescaling and outsourcing of borders

00:40:01.730 --> 00:40:03.663
is not only happening inland,

00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:06.620
it is also happening offshore

00:40:07.610 --> 00:40:11.703
especially on deserts and islands,

00:40:12.800 --> 00:40:15.030
where high measures of enclosure

00:40:16.420 --> 00:40:19.840
aim at shutting down paths to asylum

00:40:19.840 --> 00:40:21.500
but containing people

00:40:21.500 --> 00:40:24.310
trying to reach sovereign territories

00:40:24.310 --> 00:40:27.823
in M smaller and more remote locations.

00:40:29.290 --> 00:40:30.710
Such is notably the case

00:40:30.710 --> 00:40:32.893
with Europe, Southern peripheries.

00:40:35.316 --> 00:40:38.950
In particular, the two Spanish enclaves

00:40:38.950 --> 00:40:40.843
of Ceuta and Melilla.

00:40:43.850 --> 00:40:46.960
In Melilla, following the arrival

00:40:46.960 --> 00:40:50.200
of the first group of African migrants,

00:40:50.200 --> 00:40:53.437
Spain built a three metre high fence,

00:40:53.437 --> 00:40:57.238
12 kilometres square in Melilla

00:40:57.238 --> 00:41:01.122
which is a 12 kilometer square and cleave,

00:41:01.122 --> 00:41:03.740
which is located in fact

00:41:03.740 --> 00:41:06.270
in northeastern Morocco

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:09.370
which has been under Spanish sovereignty

00:41:09.370 --> 00:41:12.073
since the late 15th century.

00:41:13.640 --> 00:41:16.050
Few years later, the EU financed

00:41:16.050 --> 00:41:18.330
the construction of a six metre high,

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:21.500
three-tier fence, equipped

00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:23.780
with manned control posts,

00:41:23.780 --> 00:41:27.200
infrared cameras, acoustic sensors

00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:29.830
and motion detectors.

00:41:29.830 --> 00:41:32.580
The EU also put pressure on Morocco

00:41:32.580 --> 00:41:34.973
to control its side of the border.

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:39.730
In response, Morocco initiated

00:41:39.730 --> 00:41:42.480
regular military cleanup operations,

00:41:42.480 --> 00:41:45.110
most of which consisted in

00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:47.630
putting migrants in tracks,

00:41:47.630 --> 00:41:51.223
take them to the city of Oujda,

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:55.570
further down south near
the border with Algeria

00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:58.433
and dumping them there or in the desert.

00:42:01.731 --> 00:42:05.040
The key component of the
strategy of outsourcing,

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:07.560
European border control

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:10.740
has expanded outwardly with the assistance

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:13.480
of third countries, at the same time

00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:16.600
as internal border control

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:20.060
is strengthened through
surveillance agencies,

00:42:20.060 --> 00:42:22.450
increased rates of deportation

00:42:22.450 --> 00:42:25.693
and the proliferation
of internment centers.

00:42:27.440 --> 00:42:30.220
As far as outsourcing is concerned,

00:42:30.220 --> 00:42:33.150
the Moroccan government has been building

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:35.820
its own barbed wire fence,

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:37.650
a fence partially financed

00:42:37.650 --> 00:42:41.050
by the EU subsidies and allocated to Spain

00:42:41.050 --> 00:42:42.840
for border control.

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:45.560
The same thing is happening as we speak

00:42:45.560 --> 00:42:47.520
throughout the Sahara Desert,

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:51.700
in places such as Niger, Libya of course,

00:42:51.700 --> 00:42:55.623
Burkina Faso and along
the coast of Senegal.

00:42:58.160 --> 00:42:59.870
Typical of the distinctly

00:42:59.870 --> 00:43:03.470
neoliberal articulation
between securitization

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:06.330
and free-market internationalism,

00:43:06.330 --> 00:43:09.090
the same countries that are determined

00:43:09.090 --> 00:43:10.743
to secure their borders,

00:43:12.090 --> 00:43:14.960
are actively engaged in the construction

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:16.980
of free trade partnerships

00:43:16.980 --> 00:43:18.960
with the very same neighbors

00:43:18.960 --> 00:43:21.423
from whom they seek to protect themselves.

00:43:22.300 --> 00:43:24.130
Which raises of course

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:27.584
the question of selective permeability,

00:43:27.584 --> 00:43:29.010
in other words

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:35.133
of whose interest the opening
or closing of border serves.

00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:39.240
I could go on and on the case

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:42.290
of these two enclaves and Islands

00:43:42.290 --> 00:43:44.573
or talk a bit more about the deserts,

00:43:45.977 --> 00:43:48.900
but the point I really want to make

00:43:49.740 --> 00:43:54.740
is that the three strands, I have evoked

00:43:55.640 --> 00:43:57.773
all point towards one thing.

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:01.510
They point towards the fact that

00:44:01.510 --> 00:44:05.810
reworking and rescaling of borders

00:44:05.810 --> 00:44:07.063
is in the making.

00:44:08.090 --> 00:44:09.600
And it is in the making

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:12.233
at a planetary level, globally.

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:17.740
It is in the making as a result

00:44:17.740 --> 00:44:21.163
of the emergence of a new mobility regime,

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:24.860
one which is planetary in scale.

00:44:24.860 --> 00:44:27.520
And I would now like to say a few words

00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:31.963
about these new mobility regime.

00:44:36.570 --> 00:44:39.870
I have gone to some length describing

00:44:39.870 --> 00:44:41.870
the ways in which Islands

00:44:41.870 --> 00:44:46.760
enter into these new calculus of mobility,

00:44:46.760 --> 00:44:50.040
I also alluded to the extent to which

00:44:50.040 --> 00:44:54.860
deserts are implicated
in the same process.

00:44:54.860 --> 00:44:57.210
I would now like to focus

00:44:57.210 --> 00:44:59.170
on what we should more broadly call

00:45:00.010 --> 00:45:01.703
the liquid frontier.

00:45:04.170 --> 00:45:08.190
Because the liquid frontier is

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:12.510
at the vanguard of the
redefinition of sovereignty

00:45:12.510 --> 00:45:13.863
in the contemporary world.

00:45:15.570 --> 00:45:19.700
In order to understand
the liquid frontier,

00:45:19.700 --> 00:45:22.720
and how it reorganizes sovereignty

00:45:22.720 --> 00:45:24.920
and contributes to the making

00:45:24.920 --> 00:45:27.960
of a new global mobility regime,

00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:30.930
an excursion through Carl Schmitt's

00:45:30.930 --> 00:45:33.363
Nomos of The Earth, is necessary.

00:45:36.180 --> 00:45:38.863
If only because for Schmitt,

00:45:40.530 --> 00:45:42.810
the fundamental distinction on which

00:45:43.880 --> 00:45:48.500
geopolitics has been predicated.

00:45:48.500 --> 00:45:50.420
Geopolitics in the sense of

00:45:51.470 --> 00:45:53.850
the distribution of the earth,

00:45:53.850 --> 00:45:55.720
and the distribution of the earth

00:45:55.720 --> 00:45:58.140
which are equated with mobility

00:45:58.140 --> 00:46:00.680
and borders at the beginning.

00:46:00.680 --> 00:46:02.510
Evenly because for him,

00:46:02.510 --> 00:46:04.570
geopolitics has been predicated

00:46:05.930 --> 00:46:09.850
on a binary division, between a solid land

00:46:10.810 --> 00:46:15.000
where territories can
be clearly demarcated

00:46:15.000 --> 00:46:18.283
and where order may be imposed,

00:46:19.120 --> 00:46:20.140
and the sea.

00:46:20.140 --> 00:46:24.410
So land on the one hand,
the sea on the other

00:46:24.410 --> 00:46:27.520
where borders according to Schmidt,

00:46:27.520 --> 00:46:31.530
in any case can be neither traced

00:46:31.530 --> 00:46:36.090
nor held, and where in Schmitt's imaginary

00:46:37.310 --> 00:46:39.700
freedom reigns absolute.

00:46:39.700 --> 00:46:43.260
That's how he understand, the sea

00:46:44.810 --> 00:46:48.550
Of course this is an
idealization of the sea.

00:46:48.550 --> 00:46:53.550
Sea as an empty, almost abstract

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:56.633
and frictionless geometric space,

00:46:58.020 --> 00:47:00.643
open to navigation.

00:47:01.764 --> 00:47:04.370
A question of navigation
which we found too

00:47:04.370 --> 00:47:05.950
when I was referring to

00:47:06.860 --> 00:47:09.170
the carving of of Africa

00:47:09.170 --> 00:47:10.873
during the Berlin conference.

00:47:12.070 --> 00:47:15.923
So this idealization of
course, it has been critiqued.

00:47:17.530 --> 00:47:19.833
In fact, as we not now realize,

00:47:20.840 --> 00:47:23.923
oceans hardly cover space evenly.

00:47:27.350 --> 00:47:30.490
Oceans are in fact a fabric,

00:47:30.490 --> 00:47:31.773
that is full of holes,

00:47:32.887 --> 00:47:35.493
it is stitched together out of pieces.

00:47:36.550 --> 00:47:40.290
The sea is if we want a tangle of strings,

00:47:40.290 --> 00:47:41.930
that we know now.

00:47:41.930 --> 00:47:44.170
Schmitt might not have known about this

00:47:44.170 --> 00:47:46.360
but at least now we have the advantage

00:47:46.360 --> 00:47:49.313
of knowing better than he did.

00:47:52.870 --> 00:47:55.610
But the liquid territory
of the sea nevertheless

00:47:55.610 --> 00:47:58.293
offers a number of particularities.

00:48:02.160 --> 00:48:03.680
First, because

00:48:06.700 --> 00:48:11.623
water covers over 70% of the
surface area of our planet,

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:15.500
many frontiers in the world today

00:48:15.500 --> 00:48:19.643
are located in the sea, first point.

00:48:21.750 --> 00:48:22.583
Second,

00:48:25.450 --> 00:48:28.080
the sea is

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:32.100
what we can get

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:37.100
in terms of the idea of
an absolute boundary.

00:48:37.300 --> 00:48:39.000
All boundaries are relative,

00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:42.950
but if we were to imagine,
if only for a second,

00:48:42.950 --> 00:48:46.870
what an absolute boundary might look like,

00:48:46.870 --> 00:48:50.963
we have to look not on
land but on the sea.

00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:55.850
It is the only absolute boundary

00:48:55.850 --> 00:48:57.733
because it alone,

00:48:59.240 --> 00:49:02.280
blocks the continuous

00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:07.453
expansion of other people, it alone.

00:49:08.620 --> 00:49:11.030
Deleuze and Guattari
believed that the desert

00:49:11.030 --> 00:49:13.540
does that better than the sea,

00:49:13.540 --> 00:49:17.653
but these are discussions
we can explore later.

00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:23.550
So the sea does that, and
yet it can be domesticated

00:49:23.550 --> 00:49:25.373
to bring people into contact.

00:49:26.750 --> 00:49:29.520
It enables circulation,

00:49:29.520 --> 00:49:32.300
while adding friction to movement,

00:49:32.300 --> 00:49:34.263
while adding friction to life.

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:40.330
The other interesting thing with the sea

00:49:40.330 --> 00:49:42.120
I lay down all of these

00:49:42.120 --> 00:49:44.430
and then I would draw the
conclusion at the end,

00:49:44.430 --> 00:49:46.113
and you see where I'm going.

00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:49.310
The third interesting thing with the sea

00:49:51.370 --> 00:49:52.463
according to Schmitt,

00:49:53.960 --> 00:49:55.940
is that of course

00:49:55.940 --> 00:49:59.173
it is stirred by currents and waves,

00:50:00.060 --> 00:50:04.580
and by being stirred by currents and waves

00:50:04.580 --> 00:50:07.480
it seems to erase any trace

00:50:08.380 --> 00:50:10.950
including traces of the past,

00:50:10.950 --> 00:50:15.133
or to relegate and contain
them and it's very bottom.

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:19.840
And because of these

00:50:19.840 --> 00:50:23.630
apparent permanent erasure of all traces,

00:50:23.630 --> 00:50:27.980
the sea seems to maintain everything

00:50:27.980 --> 00:50:31.253
in a kind of permanent
present according to Schmitt.

00:50:34.300 --> 00:50:37.150
Furthermore, still with Schmitt,

00:50:37.150 --> 00:50:40.250
part of what characterizes
this vast expanse

00:50:40.250 --> 00:50:44.050
is the lack of stable
habitation on its surface.

00:50:44.050 --> 00:50:46.190
I mean this was probably before

00:50:46.190 --> 00:50:49.953
the oil digging,

00:50:51.220 --> 00:50:52.063
oil extraction,

00:50:53.360 --> 00:50:54.860
which today allows for,

00:50:54.860 --> 00:50:57.410
I mean in habitations on the surface,

00:50:57.410 --> 00:50:59.880
but we are not yet there with Schmitt

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:01.610
he's still writing at the time,

00:51:01.610 --> 00:51:04.453
when this doesn't seem at all possible.

00:51:05.430 --> 00:51:10.430
And because of this lack of
stable habitation I quote him.

00:51:10.547 --> 00:51:15.117
"Events at sea, occur mostly
outside of the public gaze

00:51:15.117 --> 00:51:18.534
"and thus remain unaccounted for."

00:51:18.534 --> 00:51:20.134
There's a lack of accountability

00:51:21.040 --> 00:51:24.930
as a characteristic feature

00:51:24.930 --> 00:51:27.053
of what happens at sea.

00:51:29.220 --> 00:51:32.540
Even more importantly,
throughout modernity

00:51:32.540 --> 00:51:34.730
maritime governance,

00:51:34.730 --> 00:51:37.290
has always oscillated between two pools.

00:51:37.290 --> 00:51:40.270
On the one hand, the desire to divide up

00:51:40.270 --> 00:51:42.190
the waters of the earth,

00:51:42.190 --> 00:51:44.490
in a way that would mirror

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:47.483
the carving up of territorial
boundaries on land,

00:51:48.400 --> 00:51:52.060
and on the other hand,
the vision of the ocean

00:51:52.060 --> 00:51:54.030
has opened to free navigation,

00:51:54.030 --> 00:51:56.830
the so-called free seas.

00:51:56.830 --> 00:52:01.100
Hugo Grotius wrote about in his text

00:52:01.100 --> 00:52:02.003
Mare Liberum.

00:52:05.540 --> 00:52:06.713
Whatever the case,

00:52:10.780 --> 00:52:11.620
Smith was

00:52:15.390 --> 00:52:16.823
wrong, we've been wrong,

00:52:17.910 --> 00:52:18.793
he wasn't wrong.

00:52:22.460 --> 00:52:25.473
He got stuck let's put it like that,

00:52:28.550 --> 00:52:32.293
with his distinction,
with his Land, Sea binary.

00:52:39.410 --> 00:52:42.560
What has happened of late

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:47.390
is that the new modes

00:52:47.390 --> 00:52:49.823
of regulation of human mobility,

00:52:51.950 --> 00:52:56.330
have tried

00:52:56.330 --> 00:52:59.263
to go beyond these land, sea binary,

00:53:01.830 --> 00:53:06.830
by being attuned more than ever before

00:53:07.310 --> 00:53:11.113
to the vertical dimensions
of maritime spaces.

00:53:12.870 --> 00:53:16.420
And in so doing, we are nowadays

00:53:16.420 --> 00:53:20.110
able to decipher a much more complex

00:53:20.110 --> 00:53:22.950
form of governance, than
the simple opposition

00:53:22.950 --> 00:53:25.070
between territorial control,

00:53:25.070 --> 00:53:27.473
and deterritorialized flows.

00:53:28.690 --> 00:53:30.323
What I mean by this is that,

00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:35.333
in the new regimes of mobility,

00:53:39.800 --> 00:53:43.980
many have come to the
realization that, in fact

00:53:46.370 --> 00:53:49.590
territorial control can go hand in hand

00:53:49.590 --> 00:53:52.113
with details deterritorialized flows.

00:53:58.810 --> 00:54:01.420
What we have today, therefore

00:54:01.420 --> 00:54:05.410
is a far more complex
jurisdictional regime

00:54:05.410 --> 00:54:06.510
and mode of governance

00:54:08.420 --> 00:54:10.660
in which the rights and obligations

00:54:10.660 --> 00:54:14.203
that compose modern state
sovereignty on the land,

00:54:15.380 --> 00:54:17.910
are decoupled from each other

00:54:17.910 --> 00:54:20.720
and applied to a varying degrees,

00:54:20.720 --> 00:54:24.710
depending on a spatial extent

00:54:24.710 --> 00:54:26.943
in this big issue in question.

00:54:31.300 --> 00:54:35.270
This is what Saskia Sassen calls

00:54:35.270 --> 00:54:36.563
Embundled Sovereignty.

00:54:40.150 --> 00:54:43.053
In this partial sovereignty of regime,

00:54:43.890 --> 00:54:48.510
intersect with ever
shifting lines of control

00:54:48.510 --> 00:54:51.823
in which routes and
traffic have become key.

00:54:53.730 --> 00:54:54.563
What does it mean?

00:54:54.563 --> 00:54:59.450
It means that in the making
of the new regime of mobility,

00:55:02.720 --> 00:55:05.503
motion has been put at the center.

00:55:06.590 --> 00:55:10.093
And if you want to control
the migration of people,

00:55:11.870 --> 00:55:13.860
what you have to follow

00:55:13.860 --> 00:55:16.293
as carefully as possible and trace,

00:55:18.380 --> 00:55:22.190
is the movement of people along routes

00:55:23.360 --> 00:55:26.003
and intersections along their journeys.

00:55:27.510 --> 00:55:29.280
So sovereignty and governance

00:55:30.260 --> 00:55:34.750
attempt now to be exercised
on routes and traffic

00:55:34.750 --> 00:55:37.570
to follow those routes, to police them

00:55:39.290 --> 00:55:42.030
while policing in the same way

00:55:43.780 --> 00:55:46.903
high seas are policed.

00:55:51.320 --> 00:55:52.173
So,

00:55:55.510 --> 00:55:58.810
sovereignty in this case has
to do with the negotiation

00:55:58.810 --> 00:56:01.840
of the inextricable articulation

00:56:01.840 --> 00:56:03.190
between freedom and control

00:56:03.190 --> 00:56:07.290
which is characteristic
indeed of forms of mobility,

00:56:07.290 --> 00:56:09.520
governance in neoliberal societies.

00:56:09.520 --> 00:56:13.540
And these sets of lines
they also simply coexist

00:56:13.540 --> 00:56:17.100
in fact, the carving up of
partial sovereignty regimes

00:56:17.100 --> 00:56:20.763
is the very basis for
governance in motion.

00:56:28.770 --> 00:56:32.350
I have used the sea to
show the extent to which

00:56:32.350 --> 00:56:35.890
it has become a laboratory,
in which contemporary modes

00:56:35.890 --> 00:56:38.740
of mobility control have been devised

00:56:38.740 --> 00:56:41.660
experimented with, which are now being

00:56:41.660 --> 00:56:43.970
brought to bear on the land.

00:56:43.970 --> 00:56:47.490
So what used to be done
exclusively at sea,

00:56:47.490 --> 00:56:52.490
is now done on the land.

00:56:54.530 --> 00:56:58.860
As at sea, border functions on the land

00:56:58.860 --> 00:57:01.910
have been decoupled from the limits

00:57:01.910 --> 00:57:03.650
of the territorial border,

00:57:03.650 --> 00:57:07.153
and are becoming increasingly
dispersed and mobile,

00:57:08.020 --> 00:57:10.683
able to follow ever shifting routes.

00:57:11.860 --> 00:57:16.230
That's what is the kernel

00:57:16.230 --> 00:57:18.343
of the new mobility regime.

00:57:22.720 --> 00:57:26.810
Now, one of the most
significant transformations

00:57:26.810 --> 00:57:31.810
in terms of the geopolitics
of mobility, is without that,

00:57:31.900 --> 00:57:34.940
the blurring of inside-outside,

00:57:34.940 --> 00:57:39.357
and the extent to which
borders have been externalized,

00:57:39.357 --> 00:57:40.740
and the border has become

00:57:40.740 --> 00:57:43.183
a transnational assemblage.

00:57:46.700 --> 00:57:49.270
A number of authors speak in this regard

00:57:49.270 --> 00:57:53.310
about the emergence of a
territorially extended,

00:57:53.310 --> 00:57:57.890
increasingly informal
and itinerant bordering,

00:57:57.890 --> 00:58:01.270
assemblage of institutions,
state authorities,

00:58:01.270 --> 00:58:04.330
policies that react to dynamic

00:58:04.330 --> 00:58:07.383
and turbulent migratory movements.

00:58:10.430 --> 00:58:14.270
At the same time the outsourcing,

00:58:14.270 --> 00:58:18.610
offshoring and extractive
realization of border work,

00:58:18.610 --> 00:58:21.990
has led to a special illegal stretching

00:58:21.990 --> 00:58:24.633
of the domains of migration control,

00:58:25.530 --> 00:58:27.590
and a redefinition of the jurisdictions

00:58:27.590 --> 00:58:30.580
and authorities traditionally associated

00:58:30.580 --> 00:58:31.773
with border management.

00:58:34.310 --> 00:58:38.573
We see it in places such
as Senegal, Mauritania,

00:58:39.450 --> 00:58:42.780
Niger, Burkina Faso, Morocco,

00:58:42.780 --> 00:58:44.453
Algeria, Libya,

00:58:46.980 --> 00:58:47.813
where

00:58:51.140 --> 00:58:54.690
the shifting performance
of sovereignty entails

00:58:54.690 --> 00:58:59.670
the ability for the security
forces of European countries

00:58:59.670 --> 00:59:04.670
to act in tandem with

00:59:05.260 --> 00:59:07.953
forces of these third States,

00:59:09.090 --> 00:59:12.053
in an attempt at cutting
of the roots of migration.

00:59:13.410 --> 00:59:16.240
Of course, all of this
raises all kinds of dilemmas

00:59:17.490 --> 00:59:19.293
these forms of mobile sovereignty,

00:59:20.150 --> 00:59:22.490
legal and institutional foundations

00:59:22.490 --> 00:59:26.670
for such programs remains unclear

00:59:28.090 --> 00:59:30.610
particularly with the near permanent

00:59:31.490 --> 00:59:33.940
operational border control policing

00:59:33.940 --> 00:59:35.040
for forces

00:59:38.571 --> 00:59:40.667
are involved in other states.

00:59:43.420 --> 00:59:45.583
Now, let me move on,

00:59:47.420 --> 00:59:50.543
and come back to the question of the body,

00:59:52.250 --> 00:59:53.333
with which I started.

00:59:56.990 --> 01:00:01.100
I indicated that the rescaling of borders

01:00:01.100 --> 01:00:03.950
has entailed a global expansion

01:00:03.950 --> 01:00:06.490
of security infrastructure,

01:00:06.490 --> 01:00:10.140
ranging from physical and virtual walls

01:00:10.140 --> 01:00:13.830
to the deployment of drones
and military hardware

01:00:13.830 --> 01:00:16.293
to monitor and secure space.

01:00:22.240 --> 01:00:27.240
I argued that a novel migration regime

01:00:27.530 --> 01:00:32.530
is emerging which requires,
as I tried to show

01:00:33.200 --> 01:00:35.380
through the reference to the sea

01:00:35.380 --> 01:00:39.893
which requires a nonlinear
understanding of territory.

01:00:42.730 --> 01:00:47.100
I also argued that the insight

01:00:47.100 --> 01:00:49.580
can now be outside,

01:00:49.580 --> 01:00:52.683
the sea on land, and the land on sea,

01:00:55.490 --> 01:00:58.230
and what we used to take as close

01:00:58.230 --> 01:01:00.770
can be far away and vice versa,

01:01:00.770 --> 01:01:05.420
all of this geometry is fast changing

01:01:05.420 --> 01:01:07.370
and it's changing as a consequence

01:01:09.470 --> 01:01:11.000
to old models of sovereignty

01:01:12.220 --> 01:01:14.530
understood at an earlier period

01:01:14.530 --> 01:01:18.350
as control of people, taxes,

01:01:18.350 --> 01:01:20.283
and discrete territories.

01:01:23.310 --> 01:01:25.610
What I would like to add now

01:01:25.610 --> 01:01:27.920
as do with the extent to which

01:01:27.920 --> 01:01:31.203
in a world of technology enabled flows,

01:01:33.070 --> 01:01:36.100
sovereignty over discrete territories

01:01:36.100 --> 01:01:39.223
has become almost by
definition incomplete.

01:01:42.110 --> 01:01:44.150
Many governments especially in the North

01:01:44.150 --> 01:01:45.520
have understood that,

01:01:45.520 --> 01:01:47.180
in order to govern the movement

01:01:47.180 --> 01:01:49.610
of people across borders,

01:01:49.610 --> 01:01:53.143
it is absolutely crucial
to first classify them.

01:01:55.580 --> 01:01:58.630
Today one, of the most efficient way

01:01:58.630 --> 01:02:00.700
to achieve such a goal,

01:02:00.700 --> 01:02:04.490
is through the harvesting
and strategic use

01:02:04.490 --> 01:02:06.310
of electronic personal data.

01:02:08.220 --> 01:02:11.190
The purpose of classification

01:02:11.190 --> 01:02:13.100
is to bring back bodies

01:02:13.100 --> 01:02:16.833
into a zone of calculation
and manageability.

01:02:19.600 --> 01:02:20.600
This is the reason why

01:02:20.600 --> 01:02:25.170
in the new regime of human
mobility I'm referring to,

01:02:25.170 --> 01:02:29.310
bordering practices have become digitized

01:02:29.310 --> 01:02:33.463
through the automation of data
collection and data analysis.

01:02:34.340 --> 01:02:37.220
So what is being collected,
genetic information

01:02:38.468 --> 01:02:41.080
it stored in electronic form

01:02:41.080 --> 01:02:45.720
in searchable bases,
physiological features

01:02:45.720 --> 01:02:49.943
unique to individuals
such as fingerprints,

01:02:50.830 --> 01:02:53.473
patterns of the iris and the retina.

01:02:54.744 --> 01:02:55.783
Veins of the hand,

01:02:57.680 --> 01:03:01.280
physionomic features
such as voice patterns

01:03:01.280 --> 01:03:06.090
are transformed via algorithms,

01:03:06.090 --> 01:03:09.710
to produce so-called templates.

01:03:09.710 --> 01:03:12.990
These templates in turn are stored

01:03:14.020 --> 01:03:17.260
in centralized databases,

01:03:17.260 --> 01:03:18.320
that are accessed

01:03:22.140 --> 01:03:23.090
whenever necessary.

01:03:24.960 --> 01:03:29.960
And templates are,

01:03:30.370 --> 01:03:32.860
when they are not stored centrally,

01:03:32.860 --> 01:03:35.480
they are put on a chip,

01:03:35.480 --> 01:03:38.410
inserted into all kinds of documents

01:03:38.410 --> 01:03:40.950
such as passport and identicals.

01:03:42.060 --> 01:03:45.350
So in this context to classify,

01:03:45.350 --> 01:03:49.220
consists in converging the data

01:03:49.220 --> 01:03:54.070
from integrated bases,
with biometric identifiers

01:03:54.920 --> 01:03:57.823
such as those that I have mentioned.

01:03:58.990 --> 01:04:03.490
And classification in this instance,

01:04:03.490 --> 01:04:08.490
relies obviously on a
series of dividing practices

01:04:09.130 --> 01:04:13.620
in which the subject is broken up

01:04:13.620 --> 01:04:16.493
into calculable risk factors.

01:04:17.540 --> 01:04:19.713
In other words to classify,

01:04:21.070 --> 01:04:25.640
which is a very old exercise
but to classify today,

01:04:25.640 --> 01:04:28.670
is to categorize populations

01:04:28.670 --> 01:04:31.363
into degrees of riskiness,

01:04:32.440 --> 01:04:37.440
and classification mostly has to do

01:04:37.500 --> 01:04:42.363
with a pre-emptive fixing of identities.

01:04:46.530 --> 01:04:50.340
So, thanks to the biometric turn,

01:04:50.340 --> 01:04:54.750
governments are embedding
the regulatory functions

01:04:55.990 --> 01:04:59.950
in various networks of global flows,

01:04:59.950 --> 01:05:03.670
with the help of digital technologies

01:05:03.670 --> 01:05:07.600
so that sovereignty, prerogatives,

01:05:07.600 --> 01:05:09.653
can travel with the flows.

01:05:12.600 --> 01:05:14.230
So to old models

01:05:16.830 --> 01:05:19.800
based on the actual physical control

01:05:19.800 --> 01:05:21.470
of discrete territories,

01:05:21.470 --> 01:05:24.450
they are grafting a new model

01:05:24.450 --> 01:05:26.713
of mobile and flowing sovereignty.

01:05:28.410 --> 01:05:32.180
In this new model, linear border functions

01:05:32.180 --> 01:05:35.990
are dislocated and pushed both outward

01:05:35.990 --> 01:05:39.070
into as I said other states territories,

01:05:39.070 --> 01:05:43.123
and inward, into national societies.

01:05:44.580 --> 01:05:48.880
Now, this new border regime
moving towards the end now

01:05:49.930 --> 01:05:54.563
has produced several
significant spatial outcomes.

01:05:56.760 --> 01:05:58.940
Borders have become more mobile

01:06:00.140 --> 01:06:03.363
but more importantly borders
have become portable.

01:06:05.620 --> 01:06:10.620
Thus, opening the entire
space of the globe

01:06:10.810 --> 01:06:13.493
to potential bordering processes.

01:06:14.410 --> 01:06:15.983
What I mean by that is that,

01:06:18.260 --> 01:06:20.093
borders have no limits.

01:06:24.451 --> 01:06:27.267
There are no longer limits
to bordering processes.

01:06:32.603 --> 01:06:36.510
There has been an increase
in bordering instances,

01:06:36.510 --> 01:06:41.510
as people can now encounter
borders in various forms

01:06:42.250 --> 01:06:46.083
in a multitude of locales
within their daily lives.

01:06:50.610 --> 01:06:52.240
The nature of an individual's encounter

01:06:52.240 --> 01:06:55.050
with borders has also changed,

01:06:55.050 --> 01:07:00.050
since this is often mediated
by diverse digital devices

01:07:00.190 --> 01:07:03.943
and managed more and
more by private actors.

01:07:05.950 --> 01:07:10.160
So, border control has
become more individualized,

01:07:10.160 --> 01:07:13.060
more molecular almost similar

01:07:13.960 --> 01:07:16.500
as digitization allows surveillance

01:07:16.500 --> 01:07:21.500
to zero in on the smaller
spaces such as the body,

01:07:21.640 --> 01:07:24.233
and continuously monitor the body.

01:07:29.057 --> 01:07:31.974
Now, the question is the following,

01:07:34.040 --> 01:07:37.270
why is it that border security practices

01:07:37.270 --> 01:07:39.770
have taken such a keen interest

01:07:39.770 --> 01:07:43.400
in the connection between
the human body and identity

01:07:44.680 --> 01:07:47.990
as a means of achieving

01:07:47.990 --> 01:07:50.393
detailed control of a movement?

01:07:59.370 --> 01:08:02.980
This is the case because the human body

01:08:02.980 --> 01:08:06.130
is seen as an indisputable anchor,

01:08:06.130 --> 01:08:08.523
to which data can be safely secured.

01:08:11.010 --> 01:08:13.150
As a result, we are witnessing

01:08:13.150 --> 01:08:16.380
a gradually extending entertwinement

01:08:16.380 --> 01:08:19.010
of individual, physical characteristics

01:08:19.010 --> 01:08:21.193
with information systems.

01:08:22.150 --> 01:08:26.470
The process that has served
to deepen faith in data,

01:08:26.470 --> 01:08:28.490
as a means of risk management,

01:08:28.490 --> 01:08:30.220
and faith in the body

01:08:30.220 --> 01:08:33.720
as a source of absolute identification.

01:08:33.720 --> 01:08:35.830
The idea is that the body remains,

01:08:35.830 --> 01:08:38.450
the truth of the individual.

01:08:38.450 --> 01:08:40.033
If we want to know who you are,

01:08:41.300 --> 01:08:42.993
we better open your body.

01:08:44.880 --> 01:08:48.710
So digital devices work they are doing

01:08:48.710 --> 01:08:51.743
is similar to work of dissection,

01:08:54.020 --> 01:08:56.290
this worries a stake in the extension

01:08:56.290 --> 01:09:01.010
of rhetoric border, into multiple organs

01:09:01.010 --> 01:09:06.010
of social life in particular
into the human body.

01:09:06.530 --> 01:09:09.780
The most efficient way
to regulate mobile bodies

01:09:09.780 --> 01:09:12.803
is to turn them into portable bodies.

01:09:15.600 --> 01:09:18.560
Let me conclude, the argument
I wanted to make tonight

01:09:18.560 --> 01:09:20.113
was pretty straightforward.

01:09:21.170 --> 01:09:24.200
The function of the border of any border,

01:09:24.200 --> 01:09:28.120
is to simultaneously divide and define,

01:09:28.120 --> 01:09:32.800
and in so doing, to generate
new sets of relationships

01:09:32.800 --> 01:09:36.810
between the social special entities

01:09:36.810 --> 01:09:39.793
and the actual bodies
they bring into existence.

01:09:41.080 --> 01:09:43.610
Borders do not simply enclose,

01:09:43.610 --> 01:09:45.780
they also provide an instrument,

01:09:45.780 --> 01:09:49.000
to gain access to, or bar access to

01:09:49.000 --> 01:09:52.363
an evenly geographically
distributed resources.

01:09:53.980 --> 01:09:55.840
As I will show tomorrow,

01:09:55.840 --> 01:09:59.150
the tension between
mobility and immobility,

01:09:59.150 --> 01:10:03.373
is itself intrinsic to the
workings of global capital.

01:10:04.840 --> 01:10:06.230
I was particularly interested

01:10:06.230 --> 01:10:08.833
in the question of the body at the border,

01:10:10.010 --> 01:10:11.423
and the borders of the body,

01:10:12.630 --> 01:10:14.823
especially that body that is not mine.

01:10:16.530 --> 01:10:20.713
Most of the bodies in motion
in our contemporary world,

01:10:22.130 --> 01:10:23.770
the multiplicity of bodies

01:10:23.770 --> 01:10:27.000
attempting to break through the wall,

01:10:27.000 --> 01:10:29.193
are engaged in an old struggle,

01:10:30.460 --> 01:10:34.683
the struggle to sustain life
under catastrophic conditions.

01:10:36.040 --> 01:10:39.720
Rendered superfluous,
by the volatile forces

01:10:39.720 --> 01:10:42.390
and flows of capital,

01:10:42.390 --> 01:10:45.420
they are desperately
trying to escape places

01:10:45.420 --> 01:10:48.113
that have been rendered uninhabitable.

01:10:50.120 --> 01:10:53.390
Catastrophic conditions should be defined,

01:10:53.390 --> 01:10:58.390
as those under which each
life and every breath

01:10:58.540 --> 01:11:02.460
suddenly acquires an utmost value,

01:11:02.460 --> 01:11:05.270
called as they are

01:11:05.270 --> 01:11:09.163
in the Nexus of
vulnerability and emergency.

01:11:10.770 --> 01:11:13.210
What a body, is and
what a body might become

01:11:14.330 --> 01:11:18.070
find the limit precisely

01:11:18.070 --> 01:11:21.853
at that border of
vulnerability and emergency.

01:11:22.950 --> 01:11:25.267
Thank you very much for your attention.

01:11:25.267 --> 01:11:28.267
(audience clapping)

01:11:40.387 --> 01:11:42.804
(soft music)

